<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  iHYPE iOS entitlements.

	  These claim capabilities that must ALSO be enabled on the App ID at
	  developer.apple.com. Both halves or neither works: an entitlement the App
	  ID does not carry makes `xcodebuild -exportArchive` fail outright, and a
	  capability on the App ID that the binary does not claim simply does
	  nothing. The App ID was registered with Push Notifications and Associated
	  Domains on 2026-09-04, which is what made this file safe to add.

	  The unsigned debug build cannot catch a mismatch here, because it builds
	  with signing disabled — so the first thing that ever checks this is the
	  signed release job.
	-->

	<!--
	  APNs environment. `development` is correct for both debug builds AND
	  TestFlight: Apple promotes the token to production automatically when the
	  build goes to the App Store, and a build claiming `production` cannot
	  receive a development push. The capability had to be on the App ID from
	  the start, because adding one later means regenerating the provisioning
	  profile.

	  This comment used to end "Nothing sends to APNs yet". That is no longer
	  true: `GoogleService-Info.plist` landed 2026-09-09, so the device now
	  registers a real token with `/api/push/register-device`, and
	  `src/lib/native-push.ts` sends to it through FCM v1 — which proxies to
	  APNs once the APNs auth key is uploaded in the Firebase console. The
	  remaining gate is not code: it is that key, plus the three
	  `FCM_*` Worker secrets the sender reads. Both are operator steps, and
	  `native-push.ts` no-ops loudly rather than failing when they are absent.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!--
	  Associated domains. Two claims on the same host, doing different jobs:

	  · `applinks:` is universal links — it is what makes an ihype.org URL open
	    the APP rather than Safari, for the paths listed in
	    src/app/.well-known/apple-app-site-association/route.ts. This is not
	    cosmetic any more: the store-review sign-in link and every emailed
	    magic link have to land inside the app, and without this they land in
	    Safari with the app still signed out.

	  · `webcredentials:` is what lets a passkey work in the WebView at all.
	    Without it WKWebView has no associated domain to scope a credential to,
	    so `navigator.credentials.get()` finds nothing, and the app's only
	    other sign-in is the magic link above.

	  No `?mode=developer` suffix. That variant tells the device to skip
	  Apple's CDN and fetch the association file directly, which is a debugging
	  aid — it also requires Developer Mode on the device and silently changes
	  where iOS looks. Ship the plain form; if verification needs debugging,
	  add it temporarily and take it out again.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:ihype.org</string>
		<string>webcredentials:ihype.org</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
